    {
      "type": "text",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 9323779231,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "text-18df2e7580b8",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": 443.851613912641,
      "y": -127.78779684768335,
      "strokeColor": "#1E40AF",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "width": 478.79999999999995,
      "height": 332.5,
      "seed": 318792,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "How It Works\nKafka provides a durable, shared event log between\nsystems, so producers and consumers do not need to be\nonline at the same moment and multiple consumers can\nindependently use or replay the same data. In an IoT\nsetup, multiple sensors send measurements over local\ndevice protocols to an edge device. A gateway application\nrunning on that edge device collects, filters,\nnormalizes, batches, or aggregates the readings, then\nuses a Kafka client to publish records to a topic such as\n`sensor-readings`. Kafka brokers append each record to a\npartition, retain it, and replicate it as configured. An\nalerting service, time-series database sink, or data-lake\nloader consumes the topic when ready. Kafka does not\nautomatically choose a destination: that work belongs to\na consumer application or Kafka Connect sink. The brokers\nrun centrally as a self-managed or managed cluster, while\nthe edge device and destination only need client or\nconnector software.",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "baseline": 17.5,
      "containerId": "node-18df2e7580b8",
      "originalText": "How It Works\nKafka provides a durable, shared event log between systems, so producers and consumers do not need to be online at the same moment and multiple consumers can independently use or replay the same data. In an IoT setup, multiple sensors send measurements over local device protocols to an edge device. A gateway application running on that edge device collects, filters, normalizes, batches, or aggregates the readings, then uses a Kafka client to publish records to a topic such as `sensor-readings`. Kafka brokers append each record to a partition, retain it, and replicate it as configured. An alerting service, time-series database sink, or data-lake loader consumes the topic when ready. Kafka does not automatically choose a destination: that work belongs to a consumer application or Kafka Connect sink. The brokers run centrally as a self-managed or managed cluster, while the edge device and destination only need client or connector software.",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "how-kafka-works"
      }
    },
